.Dd March 2026
.Dt JBT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jbt
.Nd create a .jbt/.orb/.rb archive by encrypting files with BFCodec
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl K Ar key | Fl -key Ar key | Fl -key-file Ar path
.Op Fl -iv Ar iv | Fl -iv-file Ar path
.Ar indir
.Ar archive
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
creates a ZIP-based archive
.Pa .jbt ,
.Pa .rb ,
or
.Pa .orb
by recursively reading all regular files under
.Ar indir ,
encrypting each file with the BFCodec cipher (Blowfish-derived, CBC),
and storing the encrypted data plus an 8-byte trailer (original size and
block size) in the archive under the same relative path names.
.Pp
Key and IV are required.
They can be given as hex strings
.Pq Fl K Ns / Ns Fl -key ,
.Fl -iv
(spaces ignored) or as raw binary files
.Pq Fl -key-file ,
.Fl -iv-file .
For key and IV files, only the first 16 and 8 bytes are used, respectively; files may be longer.
The IV as hex must be 16 hex digits (8 bytes).
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl K , Fl -key Ar key
Use
.Ar key
as the cipher key (hex string; spaces ignored).
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl -key-file .
.It Fl -key-file Ar path
Read the key from the binary file
.Ar path
(first 16 bytes used; file may be longer).
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl -key .
.It Fl -iv Ar iv
Use
.Ar iv
as the initialization vector (16 hex digits = 8 bytes; spaces ignored).
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl -iv-file .
.It Fl -iv-file Ar path
Read the IV from the binary file
.Ar path
(first 8 bytes used; file may be longer).
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl -iv .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr unjbt 1
